            <ArticleTitle>Pulmonary adiaspiromycosis in the Eurasian beaver (Castor fiber) inhabiting Poland.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>615-617</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1515/pjvs-2017-0077</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">/j/pjvs.2017.20.issue-3/pjvs-2017-0077/pjvs-2017-0077.xml</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Adiaspiromycosis is a rare fungal infection caused by saprophytic fungi Emmonsia spp. (type Ascomycota) occurring especially in small free-living mammals. The aim of this study was to evaluate the occurrence of histopathological lesions asscociated with adiaspiromycosis in the Eurasian beaver inhabiting Poland. In order to evaluate the presence of natural adiaspiromycosis we systematically investigated beaver populations from north-eastern Poland for adiaspores in the lungs. This study reveals for the first time the presence of pulmonary adiaspiromycosis of Eurasian beaver in Poland. As far as we know, there is no published data regarding pulmonary adiaspiromycosis in human patients in Poland.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
